        <body>
            <div type="edition" subtype="primary" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="la" resp="#TA #JP" source="#autopsy">
                <ab>
                    <lb n="1"/><gap reason="lost" extent="unknown" unit="character" n="5"/> <name><w n="10">Pition</w></name>
                    <lb n="2"/><gap reason="lost" extent="unknown" unit="character" n="15"/> <w n="20"><supplied reason="lost" cert="low">Syracu</supplied>sanus</w> <w n="25"><expan><abbr>d</abbr><ex cert="low">edit</ex></expan></w>
                </ab>
            </div>
            <div type="edition" subtype="simple-lemmatized" resp="#RC">
                <ab>
                    <gap reason="lost" extent="unknown" unit="character" n="5"><desc>[-?-]</desc></gap>
                    <w n="10" lemma="Pition">Pition</w>
                    <gap reason="lost" extent="unknown" unit="character" n="15"><desc>[-?-]</desc></gap>
                    <w n="20" lemma="Syracusanus">Syracusanus</w>
                    <w n="25" lemma="do">dedit</w>
                </ab>
            </div>
            <div type="apparatus" resp="#JP">
                <listApp>
                    <app>
                        <note>Text from autopsy</note>
                    </app>
                    <app loc="2"><note>Manganaro suggested: [salvus et] sanus d(edicavit); Degrassi in CIL: [Syracu?]sanus d(onum) [d(at)]. Due to the weathering it is impossible to know if there is an interpunct in line 2, but the D is clearly spaced from the prededing letters and the margin.</note></app>
                </listApp>
            </div>
            <div type="translation" xml:lang="en" resp="#JP">
                <p>[---]Pition [----] the Syracusan gave (this) [---]</p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary" resp="#JP">
                <p>The text comes from the main public area of the agora/forum, and by its physical form would appear to be part of the base for a statue or some other object. The right margin of the block is preserved, and so may constitute the right margin of the text also, but how much is lost to the left is unknowable. Degrassi in CIL assumed at least one further letter to the right of line 2, but this seems far from certain. Manganaro offered an entirely speculative restoration proposing a dedication to Isis, by a Roman negotiator with the cognomen Pition. Korhonen and Tribulato both note the existence of the Greek name Πιτὶων. However as Manganaro noted, the existence of Pitio as a cognomen in the Roman world is already attested by the (otherwise unknown) Roman moneyer of 148 BCE, L. Sempronius Pitio (Crawford, RRC no.216). The possibility is therefore either a Roman citizen with the Greek cognomen Pitio(n) (so Manganaro); or, as Tribulato and Korhonen suggest, assuming that line 2 should read '[Syracu]sanus', the interesting and for Sicily highly unusual possibility of a Greek citizen of Syracuse erecting a text in Latin. The very open 'P' in line 1, and the overall style of the lettering strongly suggests a Republican date in the second or (earlier) first centry BCE.</p>
            </div>
